offline
- an update shows as a dot on the menu button and a first item in the menu, and on the About
  page (/about: version, check for updates, install, offline, storage) (src/js/app-update.ts: a
  new worker waits, the page reloads only when the user chooses). Left: a slim "A new version is
  ready: Reload / Later" bar on pages that are not a setlist. Another window of the app that a
  new worker takes over keeps its old code until reloaded: not handled. Idea for About: a list
  of the errors and CSP violations seen on this device, with a Copy button, for a device whose
  DevTools are awkward (a ChromeOS tablet).

storage/sync
- `hashText` (utils/hash.ts), which sync compares to tell an edit, is a 32-bit hash: a collision is a
  missed change. A stronger one (SHA-256) means a migration of the hashes kept on records
- read-only spaces: local edits work until the leader changes the setlist (then theirs replaces the reader's, with a
  toast saying what changed, with Undo); a changed chart is held back (Reset from Drive is its revert). The sync that
  brings the leader's changes while the app is open is built (docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md, "Pulling while the app is
  open": the incremental sync, its timers, the members clock, applying a pulled setlist to the open page; no faster
  poll for the live setlist: the live channel, a WebSocket relay, is the fast way: docs/LIVE_SYNC.md); team members get the feed through the
  Worker too. The Worker role replaces the Drive-capabilities
  trigger later (docs/SPACES.md)
- a song's lead sheet range (docs/RANGE.md, docs/LEAD_SHEETS.md) is built end to end: read from a SongSelect lead
  sheet, kept in the chart as `{x_chordless_range}`, marked on the key picker, shown in the song info and settable
  by hand. Not built: the limits (`RANGE_LIMITS`) as a preference, so a group of altos or a younger group can have
  their own
- variants and editing a chart: plan in docs/VARIANTS.md (origin on each variant, the space's default, a chart editor for fixes and arrangements; the picker's cards and the versions list are built)
- conflict resolution (currently Drive wins when edited on two devices, and the local edit is
  silently dropped). Idea: keep the losing edit as a conflict copy of the setlist instead.
  Left for now: with immediate sync of the changed record (below) two devices rarely edit the
  same setlist from the same base, so it matters much less. The random sync tests (`conflicts`
  mode, tests/drive-sync-random.test.ts) already check convergence, a safety net for this change
- several people editing a setlist (designed 2026-10-16 in docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md, "Several
  people editing a setlist"; not built). Decided: start by blocking the sync of writes from
  non-owners (only the owner's edits to a setlist are uploaded); their edits stay on their device
  in a layer over the shared setlist, which needs building first (same layer as the read-only
  item under "editing"). Later: an editors list, then suggestions to the owner (accept or
  reject). Decided: createdBy and ownerId (when different) both have edit rights; a space admin
  role later. Open: open to all in the space by default, or only those. The layer must
  show, simply: one indicator that something differs from the official version, and a way to view
  what (see the doc). The Worker's team service later enforces it and detects conflicts
- a change made just before the app is closed may still not reach Drive (found 2026-10-28; the person
  prepares a setlist on the desktop, closes it, opens the tablet at church, and the desktop never
  synced). Built: a page that is hidden (`visibilitychange`: a tab switch, a minimised window, an app
  switch, a closing lid) with changes not yet sent syncs at once, without the 2 s wait or the visible
  gate (auto-sync.ts, docs/SYNC_ARCHITECTURE.md). A dropped send leaves the record "changed since last
  sync" (its content hash differs from `syncedContentHash`): not lost, sent by the next sync. Left:
  1. `#unsent` is in memory only, so a change left by a failed sync before a reload is not sent on
     hiding: also ask the database if a record differs from what was synced
  2. make the upload survive the page going: `fetch` with `keepalive` for the small JSON writes (a
     setlist, a song file), which the browser may finish after the page is gone. No guarantee: a Drive
     upload takes several requests and a token, a keepalive body is limited to 64 KB, and iPad Safari
     is the weakest. Do it only if hiding is not enough
  3. ask before closing with unsent changes: a `beforeunload` prompt while `#unsent` is true or a
     record differs from what was synced. It is friction (the rule in CLAUDE.md: friction, not
     prohibition), and the browser shows its own text; a backstop
  4. show it: a cue that there are changes not yet sent (and when it last synced), in the header or on
     the Sync button, if the app has none yet (check what the Storage page and the space card already
     show), so the person can see before closing
  Nothing here is reliable on a page being torn down: 1 lowers the risk, and looking for "synced" before
  closing removes it. To learn how often it happens: `chordless.setlist.unsent_on_leave` with
  `chordless.setlist.left = page` on the leave span (built: docs/OBSERVABILITY.md)
- fewer Drive calls in a full sync (what the traces showed, 2026-10-29: a manual sync made 17; now about
  8: the feed's no-op, the repeated inventory, the serial listings, the pull's listings and the folder
  lookups are fixed: docs/OBSERVABILITY.md). Left: an incremental sync that finds changes asks for the
  access before it knows it will write: it could wait for a write that needs it
- privacy-preserving analytics (cookieless, with song popularity by CCLI number): the plan and its constraints are in
  docs/ANALYTICS.md
- tighten the Content-Security-Policy (drop `style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'`, Trusted Types, `upgrade-insecure-requests`,
  a `report-to`) and check the service worker's own fetches and Safari: docs/BUILD.md, "Tightening the Content-Security-Policy"
- richer PWA install UI (Chrome's devtools manifest tab warns): add "screenshots" to
  public/manifest.webmanifest, at least one with form_factor "wide" (desktop) and one without it
  or "narrow" (mobile). Generate them with Playwright from seeded data, using a public-domain
  hymn (no copyrighted lyrics), and keep them out of the precache if they are large
- proofread the sample charts (public/samples): the words were written from memory and the chords
  and the time and tempo are my guesses. Check the words against a source before real use
- check on a real iPhone/iPad (Safari): the first visit with no reload (public/boot.js waits two
  seconds for the worker to claim the page, then reloads), and the card's install instructions
- first-load speed: a cold first visit's LCP waits on one more request wave (the home view's
  chunks load after the entry runs). If it feels slow on a real device, preload the landing view
  with the entry (about 15 KB gzipped more for every route). Measure on the live site first
- Lighthouse leftovers: pinch zoom is off on purpose (accessibility flags it); the llms.txt and
  ai-catalog audits are ignored
- test song import on real devices, on the live site: file import and paste on Android and iPad,
  the bookmarklet on Android Chrome, and whether the phone/tablet tip shows on the bookmarklet page
- user-facing Drive health check / cleanup (idea): turn src/dev/drive-audit.html into an
  app feature that finds duplicate or empty setlists and stray space folders, and
  moves them to the Drive trash after confirmation. Would need songs covered too.

